1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
In this tutorial you'll learn about the main features of the sitemap and how to use them in combination with other tools.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Open Burps browser and use it to access the following lab.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
The lab is entitled, Excessive Trust in Client Site Controls.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Click Access the lab and login to your Portswigger account if prompted.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:32,000
This opens your own instance of a deliberately vulnerable website.

6
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:36,000
In Burp, go to the Target Sitemap tab.

7
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Then expand the contents of the node that represents the vulnerable lab website.

8
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:45,000
As you can see, Burp has already started to populate the sitemap.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,000
Notice that some items are greyed out.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,000
Burp predicts that these items exist, but your browser hasn't completed a request-response cycle for them yet.

11
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,000
This is probably because the URL appears in another response that you received already.

12
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,000
The sitemap updates automatically as you browse.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
If there's a particular greyed out item that you're interested in, you can copy the URL from the sitemap and paste it into Burp's browser.

14
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
The sitemap tab contains various panels that show different information, such as the first request and response for each item.

15
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:22,000
If you're using Burp Suite Professional, you'll also see a list of any security issues the Burp Scanner identifies.

16
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Select a node in the sitemap.

17
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Notice that the information in the panels is now filtered based on the selected node.

18
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:34,000
The sitemap also makes it easy to set the target scope for Burp.

19
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Right click on the top level node for the lab and select Add to Scope.

20
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
When prompted to stop proxying out of scope traffic, click Yes.

21
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:51,000
You might want to exclude specific paths and sub-directories that you're not interested in from the scope.

22
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Right click on the node for Resources, Images and select Exclude from Scope.

23
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Now, go to the Target Scope tab.

24
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:13,000
You can see that the entire domain for the lab is in scope, but the Resources image directory is excluded.

25
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:18,000
You can also delete any items that you're not interested in from the sitemap tab.

26
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:22,000
These might be domains that were added before you set the scope.

27
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:29,000
Select the items, right click and select Delete.

